<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/advanced/spectroscopy-and-structure">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Modern chemists rarely see molecules — they <strong>interrogate them with light and
    magnets</strong>. The organising idea is an energy ladder: molecules can absorb only
    photons whose energy matches a gap between allowed states, and each rung of the ladder
    probes a different feature. Radio waves flip nuclear spins (NMR — the chemical
    neighbourhood of each hydrogen); infrared light excites bond vibrations (which bonds
    are present); visible and UV light promote electrons (conjugation and colour); and
    X-rays, too energetic to be absorbed resonantly, scatter off electrons (the full 3D
    arrangement). One molecule, four instruments, four complementary answers. Alongside
    the optical instruments sit the classical quantitative tools of the syllabus unit —
    titrations, chromatography, and wet identification tests — which answer the same
    "what is it, and how much" questions by chemistry rather than optics. A trained
    chemist reads all of them like text.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="an infrared fingerprint — the dips mark bond vibrations: O–H, C=O, C–H each singe at their own frequency" widget="plotter" config={specCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the three deep dips in the figure: ~3300 cm⁻¹ is an O–H stretch (alcohol or
    water), ~2900 cm⁻¹ the C–H backbone, ~1750 cm⁻¹ the C=O of a carbonyl. An O–H
    <em>and</em> a C=O on one molecule — this spectrum is basically vinegar introducing
    itself. The broad O–H band (spanning hundreds of wavenumbers, because hydrogen bonds
    jiggle the stiffness) is itself diagnostic: a sharp O–H says free alcohol, a broad
    one says acid or hydrogen-bonded associates.
  </p>

  <h2>Infrared: bonds as springs</h2>
  <p>
    A chemical bond vibrates like a spring, and quantum mechanics makes its vibration
    energy quantised: E = hf, with the frequency set by the bond's <strong>stiffness</strong>
    (stronger bonds vibrate faster) and the <strong>masses</strong> it connects (lighter
    atoms vibrate faster — the reduced-mass effect). The hierarchy falls out of those two
    rules: O–H and N–H stretches (light H, strong bond) sit high at 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; C–H
    near 2900; the stiff double bond C=O near 1700; floppy single bonds like C–O and C–C
    below 1300, where the crowded tangle of bands becomes the unique <strong>fingerprint
    region</strong> — compare it against a library spectrum and identity is confirmed.
    Quantitatively, a 1700 cm⁻¹ photon has energy hc·ν̄ ≈ 3.4 × 10⁻²⁰ J ≈ 0.21 eV — about
    eight times kT at room temperature, which is why thermal jostling populates the
    vibrational ground state heavily and absorption lines are clean. IR spectroscopy is
    the cheap, fast, first question asked of any new compound — and, aimed outward, the
    same technique reads planetary atmospheres (the JWST and the climate lesson's
    greenhouse gases both work in exactly these bands).
  </p>

  <h2>Mass spectrometry: weigh the molecule, weigh the pieces</h2>
  <p>
    Ionise the molecule (typically by knocking off one electron), accelerate the ions,
    and sort them by mass-to-charge ratio m/z. The heaviest significant peak — the
    <strong>molecular ion</strong> M⁺ — gives the molecular mass directly: a peak at
    m/z = 46 means ethanol, C₂H₆O, and nothing else that light. The <strong>fragmentation
    pattern</strong> below it is a fingerprint of structure: ethanol breaks predictably
    at the C–C bond (a chunk at 31, CH₂OH⁺, shouting "there is an –OH on the end"). Two
    bonus features make MS quantitative and forensic. <strong>Isotope patterns</strong>:
    chlorine's two isotopes (³⁵Cl, ³⁷Cl, 3:1) split any Cl-containing peak into a
    3:1 doublet; bromine's (⁷⁹Br, ⁸¹Br) into a 1:1 pair — instant element detection.
    <strong>High-resolution MS</strong> distinguishes 27.9949 (carbon monoxide) from
    28.0062 (nitrogen) — masses eight decimal places apart —
    which is how Apollo's soil samples, breathalysers, and sports drug testing all work.
    Couple it to gas chromatography (below) and you have GC-MS, the workhorse of
    forensics, doping control and environmental monitoring.
  </p>

  <h2>NMR: a map of every hydrogen's neighbourhood</h2>
  <p>
    Put a sample in a strong magnetic field and each ¹H nucleus (spin ½) splits into two
    energy states; flip them with a radio pulse of exactly the right frequency and
    listen. The <strong>chemical shift</strong> — how far a proton's resonance sits from
    the reference compound TMS, in ppm — reports its electronic neighbourhood, because
    surrounding electrons shield the nucleus from the field: hydrogens on carbons next to
    oxygen resonate downfield near 3–4 ppm; plain alkyl hydrogens near 1 ppm; aldehyde
    hydrogens near 10. Three further pieces of information make the map complete:
    the <strong>integral</strong> of each peak counts the hydrogens in that group; the
    <strong>splitting</strong> follows the n+1 rule (a proton's peak is split by its
    n nearest non-equivalent neighbours); and <strong>coupling patterns</strong> link the
    groups to each other. Worked example, ethanol: three peaks — a triplet of 3 units
    at ~1.2 ppm (the CH₃, split by its 2 CH₂ neighbours), a quartet of 2 units at ~3.7
    ppm (the CH₂, split by the 3 CH₃ neighbours — the triplet/quartet pairing proves
    they are bonded to each other), and a broad 1-unit OH signal that wanders with
    hydrogen bonding. That is the entire structure read out of a drop of liquid.
    <strong>MRI in hospitals is this physics aimed at water</strong> — gradient fields
    make the resonance frequency position-dependent, and the body becomes its own ¹H
    spectrum, imaged slice by slice.
  </p>

  <h2>X-ray crystallography: the structure itself</h2>
  <p>
    X-ray wavelengths (~0.1 nm) match atomic spacings, so a crystal diffracts them into
    a pattern of spots. <strong>Bragg's law, nλ = 2d·sin θ</strong>, converts spot angles
    into the distances between atomic planes; collecting thousands of reflections and
    Fourier-transforming them reconstructs the electron density — the molecule, in three
    dimensions, atoms placed directly. It is how the double helix was seen (Franklin's
    Photo 51: the cross-shaped pattern of a helix, read by Watson and Crick in 1953), how
    penicillin's strained ring was proved, and how the Protein Data Bank's ~200,000
    protein structures were obtained. Bragg in practice: with copper X-rays (λ = 0.154
    nm) and planes 0.2 nm apart, the first-order reflection sits at
    sin θ = 0.154/0.4 = 0.385, θ ≈ 23° — the arithmetic a diffractometer runs
    automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>The classical toolkit: titration, chromatography, identification (12.2–12.5)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Titrations</strong> answer "how much" to four significant figures: a standard
    solution of known concentration reacts with the unknown until an indicator flips at
    the equivalence point, and stoichiometry converts burette volumes into concentration.
    The full worked example, start to finish:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a titration calculation, end to end — the chemistry that makes every concentration in every textbook trustworthy" widget="equationStep" config={titrCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>Chromatography</strong> answers "what mixture is it": components separate
    because they spend different fractions of their time dissolved in the mobile phase
    versus stuck to the stationary phase — the better-dissolved ones travel further.
    Paper chromatography reports each spot as an <strong>R<sub>f</sub> value</strong>
    (distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent — a spot at 4.2 cm with the front
    at 7.0 cm has R<sub>f</sub> = 0.60, reproducible and comparable to libraries); gas
    chromatography does the same for volatile compounds with helium as the mobile phase;
    HPLC handles non-volatile, drug-scale molecules under pressure. Run the outlet into
    a mass spectrometer and separation plus identification happen in one instrument.
    <strong>Wet identification</strong> (12.5) closes the loop with chemistry: flame tests
    colour ions (sodium yellow, potassium lilac, copper blue-green); NaOH precipitates
    hydroxides by colour (Cu²⁺ blue, Fe³⁺ rust-brown, Fe²⁺ green); and the gases have
    signature tests — CO₂ turns limewater milky, H₂ pops with a lit splint, O₂ relights
    a glowing one, ammonia turns damp red litmus blue, chlorine bleaches damp litmus
    white.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> these techniques "see molecules" directly. None does.
    IR sees bond stiffness; NMR sees local magnetic environments; mass spectrometry sees
    mass-to-charge of ions, from which mass is inferred; even crystallography sees
    electron density, from which atom positions are modelled. Every structure ever
    "observed" is a model that best explains spectra — which is why structures get
    revised, and why convergence among independent techniques (IR says acid, NMR says
    ethyl, MS says 60, X-ray shows the geometry) is what actually establishes a
    structure.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing the separation: the purification decision tree (12.1, 12.4)</h2>
  <p>
    Every structure determination begins with a pure sample, and purity is bought with
    the right separation. The decision tree runs on the properties that differ.
    Different <em>solubility</em>: filter off the insoluble solid, or use
    <strong>recrystallisation</strong> — dissolve the crude solid hot, cool it, and let
    the desired compound crystallise while impurities (present in small amounts) stay
    dissolved; the classic purification, judged by melting point (pure solids melt
    sharply; impure ones melt low and broad). Different <em>boiling point</em>:
    distillation — simple for liquids far apart (ethanol 78 °C from water's 100 °C, though
    azeotropes cap the purity near 96%), fractionating columns for close-boiling crude
    oil. Different <em>volatility or affinity</em>: the chromatographies. Different
    <em>sublimation or magnetism or density</em>: specialised tricks, including the
    centrifuge. Good experimental design (12.1) means choosing the separation <em>and</em>
    the analysis together: purity checks (melting point, TLC single spot, a clean NMR)
    are part of the method, not an afterthought — an unverified yield is a guess with
    arithmetic on it.
  </p>

  <h2>UV-visible: electrons, conjugation and colour</h2>
  <p>
    The highest rung on the optical ladder: UV and visible photons (3–0.16 × 10⁻¹⁹ J,
    wavelengths 200–750 nm) promote electrons between molecular orbitals, and the gap
    depends on <strong>conjugation</strong> — the more alternating double bonds a
    molecule chains together, the smaller the gap and the redder the light absorbed.
    A single C=C absorbs far-UV (invisible); β-carotene's eleven conjugated bonds absorb
    blue-green, so the carrot pigment looks orange; the retina's retinal (a shorter
    conjugated chain) absorbs green, which is the photon-to-nerve event of vision. The
    workhorse quantitative law is <strong>Beer–Lambert: A = ε·c·l</strong> — absorbance
    scales with concentration and path length, with ε the molecule's characteristic
    absorptivity. Worked: a compound with ε = 50,000 L/(mol·cm) at 0.02 mmol/L in a
    1 cm cuvette reads A = 1.0 — five-decimal detection limits from a torch-bulb's worth
    of physics, which is why spectrophotometers run hospital biochemistry and water
    quality monitors alike. Colour itself is subtraction chemistry: a red shirt is a
    molecule whose gaps eat everything but red.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fourier transform methods.</strong> Modern instruments do not scan
    frequencies one at a time. FTIR hits the sample with all frequencies at once (a
    moving mirror encodes them in an interferogram) and a Fourier transform decodes the
    spectrum — the multiplex advantage that made IR routine. Pulsed NMR does the same in
    time domain: one broadband pulse excites every nucleus, and the decaying signal (the
    free induction decay) transforms into the spectrum. The same mathematics is the
    convolution calculus of the integration lesson applied to real hardware, and it is
    the reason a 20-minute NMR spectrum replaced a week of continuous-wave scanning.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Structure at the limits.</strong> Protein NMR (2D and 3D experiments —
    COSY, NOESY — cross-linking thousands of proton-pair distances) solved solution
    structures X-ray crystals cannot give; the 2017 Nobel cryo-EM revolution now freezes
    single particles and images them by electron scattering, reaching near-atomic
    resolution without any crystal at all; and X-ray free-electron lasers are pushing
    toward diffraction-before-destruction of single molecules. On the small end,
    scanning tunnelling microscopy (the quantum-foundations lesson's tunneling) images
    individual bonds directly — the first time "seeing a molecule" stopped being a
    metaphor.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quantitative structure determination and open questions.</strong> The
    frontier problems are sensitivity and speed: single-molecule mass spectrometry and
    NMR of nano-litre samples; ultrafast (femtosecond) electron diffraction watching
    bonds break in real time; and machine-learning structure prediction (AlphaFold,
    2021) which now predicts protein folds from sequence — spectacularly well, though it
    predicts <em>models</em> whose experimental validation still runs through the
    techniques on this page. The through-line: every advance in chemistry's ability to
    <em>make</em> molecules has been paired with an advance in its ability to
    <em>ask them what they are</em> — and that second engineering discipline, analytical
    and structural chemistry, is where the syllabus's experimental-techniques unit is
    heading.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pavia et al., <em>Introduction to Spectroscopy</em>; Franklin &amp; Gosling, <em>Nature</em> (1953); Protein Data Bank (rcsb.org); Cambridge IGCSE Chemistry syllabus, units 12.1–12.5.</p>
</TopicPage>
